# Rule 5 of the product direction (issue #3; F48): leave things better than you found them. Float All, Quit and a pause give
# windows back their own earlier sizes, not just a place in view.
# Story: four windows, each with a size and a place of its own, spend an hour docked, framed, in tiles and in the Dock.
# Each way out puts them back. A pause is as if Arbor had quit, and it comes back (QUESTIONS Q21).
quiet
open Safari 900x600
open Terminal 600x400
open Mail 700x500
open Notes 400x300
move 101 40 60 900 600
move 102 800 80 600 400
move 103 300 360 700 500
move 104 1000 520 400 300
# Float All, from a framed view, with one window in the Dock.
arrange
expect count docked 4
zoom 102
minimize 103
float-all
expect count docked 0
expect size 101 900x600
expect at 101 40 60
expect size 102 600x400
expect at 102 800 80
expect size 104 400x300
expect at 104 1000 520
restore 103
expect size 103 700x500
expect at 103 300 360
# One window at a time: its own size.
dock 104 left
expect docked 104
float 104
expect size 104 400x300
move 104 1000 520 400 300
# A pause: everything is as it was, the framed window too. Resuming brings arrangement and view back.
arrange
zoom 101
expect hidden 102
pause
expect moved-once
expect size 101 900x600
expect at 101 40 60
expect size 102 600x400
expect at 102 800 80
expect size 103 700x500
expect size 104 400x300
resume
expect moved-once
expect fills 101
expect hidden 102
# Quit.
quit-check
expect replied QUIT ok
expect size 101 900x600
expect at 101 40 60
expect size 102 600x400
expect at 102 800 80
expect size 103 700x500
expect at 103 300 360
expect size 104 400x300
expect at 104 1000 520
